---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code as CodeSnippet } from "astro:components";
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Section Summary: Impact of Computing</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-4" />

        <p class="text-secondary">
          Activities 3.11 &ndash; 3.18. Use this to review, or to fill in a definition you missed.
        </p>

        <hr />

        <section>
          <h2><span class="text-sn-theme">1.</span> Beneficial and Harmful Effects</h2>

          <p>
            Every innovation has both,
            at the same time, and which is which depends on context. Naming the
            effect is only half the answer &mdash; you have to justify
            it.
          </p>

          <dl>
            <dt>Computing innovation</dt>
            <dd>Something that includes a program as an integral part of its function. Physical, nonphysical software, or a nonphysical concept.</dd>
            <dt>Effect</dt>
            <dd>An impact, a result, or an outcome. Beneficial and harmful effects are contextual and interpretive.</dd>
            <dt>Identification</dt>
            <dd>Both the classification of an effect and the justification for that classification.</dd>
          </dl>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section>
          <h2><span class="text-sn-theme">2.</span> The Digital Divide</h2>

          <p>
            Access is not evenly distributed, and it
            varies with where and who you are. The part easiest to miss is
            whose voice reaches the decision.
          </p>

          <dl>
            <dt>Digital divide</dt>
            <dd>Differing access to computing devices and the internet, based on socioeconomic, geographic or demographic characteristics.</dd>
            <dt>Equity</dt>
            <dd>Whether people are given what they need to have a fair chance &mdash; not whether they are given the same thing.</dd>
            <dt>Influence</dt>
            <dd>Whose voice reaches a decision. The issue the digital divide raises that is easiest to miss.</dd>
          </dl>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section>
          <h2><span class="text-sn-theme">3.</span> Computing Bias</h2>

          <p>
            Bias enters through the algorithm or through
            the data. A program can be working exactly as written and still do
            harm.
          </p>

          <dl>
            <dt>Computing bias</dt>
            <dd>An innovation reflecting existing human bias, through its algorithms or through its data.</dd>
            <dt>Proxy variable</dt>
            <dd>A variable that stands in for something else &mdash; often for something you were not allowed to use. <b>This is our word, not the exam's</b> &mdash; the idea is examinable, the term is not.</dd>
            <dt>Unintentional bias</dt>
            <dd>Bias produced without anyone intending it. <b>Also our framing, not the exam's</b> &mdash; the exam asks where bias came from, not whether it was meant.</dd>
          </dl>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section>
          <h2><span class="text-sn-theme">4.</span> Crowdsourcing and Citizen Science</h2>

          <p>
            Many small contributions
            from many people can do work no single group could. Openness helps
            the research and the misuse equally.
          </p>

          <dl>
            <dt>Crowdsourcing</dt>
            <dd>Obtaining input or information from a large number of people via the internet.</dd>
            <dt>Citizen science</dt>
            <dd>Research conducted in whole or part by distributed individuals, many not scientists, contributing data using their own computing devices.</dd>
            <dt>Dissemination</dt>
            <dd>Spreading results. The third thing public data access makes easier.</dd>
          </dl>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section>
          <h2><span class="text-sn-theme">5.</span> Legal and Ethical Concerns</h2>

          <p>
            Material someone created belongs
            to them. Licenses are how a creator says what you may do with it,
            and citing costs you nothing.
          </p>

          <dl>
            <dt>Intellectual property</dt>
            <dd>Material created by someone, belonging to its creator or their organization.</dd>
            <dt>Plagiarism</dt>
            <dd>Using material created by someone else, without permission, presented as one's own.</dd>
            <dt>Creative Commons</dt>
            <dd>A public copyright license enabling free distribution of an otherwise copyrighted work.</dd>
            <dt>Open source</dt>
            <dd>Programs made freely available, which may be redistributed and modified.</dd>
            <dt>Open access</dt>
            <dd>Online research output free of restrictions on access and of many restrictions on use.</dd>
          </dl>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section>
          <h2><span class="text-sn-theme">6.</span> PII, Aggregation, and Privacy</h2>

          <p>
            Individually harmless details
            combine into something that identifies a person. Once information
            is out, it is difficult to remove.
          </p>

          <dl>
            <dt>PII</dt>
            <dd>Personally identifiable information &mdash; information that identifies, links, relates to, or describes an individual.</dd>
            <dt>Aggregation</dt>
            <dd>Combining disparate data to create knowledge about an individual that no single item provided.</dd>
            <dt>Curation</dt>
            <dd>The gathering and organizing of information by a commercial or governmental body.</dd>
          </dl>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section>
          <h2><span class="text-sn-theme">7.</span> Authentication and Encryption</h2>

          <p>
            Authentication is about who
            you are; encryption is about who can read it. Which key does which
            job is the distinction to keep straight.
          </p>

          <dl>
            <dt>Authentication</dt>
            <dd>Measures protecting devices and information from unauthorized access.</dd>
            <dt>Multifactor authentication</dt>
            <dd>Access granted only after presenting evidence from at least two of: knowledge, possession, inherence.</dd>
            <dt>Encryption / decryption</dt>
            <dd>Encoding data to prevent unauthorized access, and decoding it.</dd>
            <dt>Symmetric key</dt>
            <dd>One key for both encryption and decryption.</dd>
            <dt>Public key encryption</dt>
            <dd>A public key encrypts; a private key decrypts.</dd>
            <dt>Certificate authority</dt>
            <dd>Issues digital certificates validating ownership of encryption keys, based on a trust model.</dd>
          </dl>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section>
          <h2><span class="text-sn-theme">8.</span> Malware and Unauthorized Access</h2>

          <p>
            A virus is one kind of
            malware, not a synonym for it. Several of the common attacks target
            the person rather than the system.
          </p>

          <dl>
            <dt>Malware</dt>
            <dd>Software intended to damage a computing system or take partial control over its operation.</dd>
            <dt>Computer virus</dt>
            <dd>A malicious program that can copy itself and gain unauthorized access, often by attaching to legitimate programs.</dd>
            <dt>Phishing</dt>
            <dd>A technique that attempts to trick a user into providing personal information.</dd>
            <dt>Keylogging</dt>
            <dd>Using a program to record every keystroke, to gain fraudulent access to passwords and confidential information.</dd>
            <dt>Rogue access point</dt>
            <dd>A wireless access point that gives unauthorized access to secure networks.</dd>
          </dl>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  dl {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }
  dt {
    margin-top: 0.75rem;
  }
  dd {
    margin-bottom: 0;
    padding-left: 1rem;
  }
  section {
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
  }
  code {
    font-size: 1rem;
  }
</style>
